---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import Contributions from '../../components/Contributions.astro';
import PaymentDiagram from '../../components/PaymentDiagram.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === 'ticket-box')!;
const repo = 'https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/blob/develop/apps/backend-api/src';
---

<Base title="Ticket_Box case study" description={project.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <ProjectHeader project={project} />

    <nav class="toc card" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#problem">The problem</a></li>
        <li><a href="#idempotency">Not charging twice</a></li>
        <li><a href="#breaker">When the gateway is down</a></li>
        <li><a href="#workers">Heavy work off the request path</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cache">Caching and delivery</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limits">What I would change</a></li>
        <li><a href="#built">Everything I built</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="problem">
      <span class="eyebrow">01 · Context</span>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Concert ticket sales are bursty: a popular show opens and thousands of people try to pay at the same moment. Two
        things go wrong in that situation. Users double-click or their app retries, and the same payment is attempted
        twice. And the third-party gateway slows down or fails, and every request waiting on it piles up.
      </p>
      <p>
        My part of the team was the payment flow and the background work around it. The goal was simple to state: an
        order is paid <em>at most once</em>, and a gateway outage degrades the checkout instead of taking the API down
        with it.
      </p>
      <PaymentDiagram />
    </section>

    <section id="idempotency">
      <span class="eyebrow">02 · Decision</span>
      <h2>Not charging twice: two-layer idempotency</h2>
      <p>
        Every payment request must carry an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header (a UUID v4 generated by the client).
        The obvious implementation, “look the key up, and if it isn’t there, store it”, has a race: two identical
        requests arriving together both see “not there” and both create a payment. So the reservation uses Redis
        <code>SET NX</code>, which checks and writes in one atomic step.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`// payment.service.ts — processPayment (simplified)
const cached = await redis.getJson(cacheKey);
if (isCompleted(cached)) return cached.response;          // replay: same answer as before

const reserved = await redis.setIfAbsentJson(               // SET key value NX EX 86400
  cacheKey, { state: 'IN_PROGRESS', order_id }, 24 * 3600);

if (!reserved) {
  const existing = await findCachedOrPersistedResult(key); // Redis, then PostgreSQL
  if (existing) return existing;
  throw new ConflictException('Payment request is already in progress'); // 409
}
// ...load the order, call the gateway, store COMPLETED or FAILED under the same key`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Redis is fast but not the source of truth: it can be restarted or evict keys. That is why the key is also
        stored in a unique column on the payment transaction in PostgreSQL. If Redis forgets, the database still
        refuses a second transaction for the same key, and the lookup falls back to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A related case is a user who leaves checkout and comes back later. Instead of opening a second PayOS session
        (which the gateway rejects for the same order code), the service reuses the still-valid checkout link stored on
        the active transaction, and the PayOS link expiry is aligned with the order’s own expiry
        (<a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/96">#96</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="breaker">
      <span class="eyebrow">03 · Decision</span>
      <h2>When the gateway is down: a circuit breaker</h2>
      <p>
        Without protection, a slow gateway makes every checkout request hang until it times out, and those requests
        hold connections and memory the rest of the API needs. A circuit breaker watches the error rate and, once it is
        too high, fails fast for a while instead of calling the gateway at all.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`// payment-gateway.client.ts
new CircuitBreaker((input) => strategy.createPaymentSession(input), {
  timeout: 3_000,               // a call slower than 3 s counts as a failure
  errorThresholdPercentage: 50, // open when at least half of the calls fail...
  volumeThreshold: 4,           // ...but only after 4+ calls, so one error can't trip it
  rollingCountTimeout: 10_000,  // measured over a 10 s window (10 one-second buckets)
  resetTimeout: 60_000,         // after 60 s, let one trial call through (half-open)
});`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        There is one breaker per payment method, so a failing provider cannot block another one. Gateways sit behind a
        small Strategy interface (<code>createPaymentSession</code>, <code>getPaymentSession</code>,
        <code>cancelPaymentSession</code>, <code>verifyWebhookSignature</code>), which made it straightforward to
        replace the early mock VNPay/MoMo adapters with PayOS. When the breaker is open, the service
        records the failure under the idempotency key and returns <code>503</code> with the breaker state, so the
        client can show “payment is temporarily unavailable” instead of spinning.
      </p>
      <p>
        Payment results arrive later through a webhook. The handler verifies the PayOS signature, then marks the order
        paid and issues tickets, or releases the held seats if the payment failed or was cancelled
        (<a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/52">#52</a>). A scheduled job expires
        pending orders whose hold time has passed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="workers">
      <span class="eyebrow">04 · Decision</span>
      <h2>Heavy work off the request path</h2>
      <p>
        Organisers can upload a guest list as a CSV file that may be large. Parsing and inserting that
        inside an HTTP request would block it for a long time, so the endpoint only stores the file, creates a
        <code>BackgroundJob</code> row, and publishes a message to RabbitMQ. A consumer does the work and updates the
        job’s progress, which the client polls.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The CSV is <strong>streamed</strong>, not loaded into memory, and written in <strong>batches of 500</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          Each batch is one <code>INSERT … ON CONFLICT (concert_id, email) DO UPDATE</code>, so re-importing a file
          updates guests instead of failing.
        </li>
        <li>
          A real bug I hit: PostgreSQL rejects a batch with <em>“ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE command cannot affect row a
          second time”</em> when the same email appears twice <em>in the same batch</em>. The fix was to de-duplicate
          each batch before sending it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The same queue-and-worker pattern runs two other jobs I wrote: generating an artist biography from an uploaded
        PDF press kit (text extraction, cleanup, then Gemini with retry and backoff), and sending email and in-app
        notifications (<a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/63">#63</a>,
        <a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/64">#64</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cache">
      <span class="eyebrow">05 · Supporting work</span>
      <h2>Caching and delivery</h2>
      <p>
        Concert listings are read far more often than they change, so <code>GET /concerts</code> and
        <code>GET /concerts/:id</code> use cache-aside: read Redis first, fall back to PostgreSQL on a miss, then store
        the result for 24 hours. The service also survives Redis being unavailable instead of crashing
        (<a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/33">#33</a>,
        <a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/35">#35</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        I also set up the backend pipeline: on every pull request GitHub Actions runs lint, typecheck,
        <code>prisma validate</code>, a migration check against a throwaway PostgreSQL, and a build, and a multi-stage
        Docker image is deployed to Railway
        (<a href="https://github.com/CQ23-SoftwareDesign-2026/Ticket_Box/pull/18">#18</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits">
      <span class="eyebrow">06 · Honest review</span>
      <h2>What I would change</h2>
      <p>Reading my own code again after the course, these are the weaknesses I would fix first:</p>
      <ol class="limits">
        <li>
          <strong>Stale concert lists.</strong> Creating or editing a concert refreshes its detail cache but does not
          clear the cached list pages, so a list can be up to 24 hours out of date. I would version the list keys or
          delete them by prefix on every write.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stuck “in progress” keys.</strong> The <code>IN_PROGRESS</code> reservation has the same 24-hour TTL
          as a finished result. If the process crashes mid-payment, retries get <code>409</code> until the key expires
          (unless the database already has a result). A short TTL for the in-progress state, extended on completion,
          would fix this.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Breaker state per instance.</strong> The circuit state lives in memory, so with several replicas each
          one learns about an outage separately. That is acceptable at this scale, but worth knowing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not load-tested by me.</strong> This is a course project, not a production system. A teammate ran k6
          tests on reservations; I have not measured the payment path under load.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="built">
      <Contributions project={project} heading="Everything I built, with evidence" />
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .toc ol { margin: 0.5rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; columns: 2; column-gap: 32px; }
  @media (max-width: 600px) { .toc ol { columns: 1; } }
  .toc a { text-decoration: none; }
  section p, section ul, section ol { max-width: 46rem; }
  .limits li { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
</style>
